@node Gowl AI Field
@chapter Gowl AI Field: AI Commands on Any Text Field

@cindex AI field
@cindex text field, AI commands on
@cindex text-input-v3
@cindex gowl, text fields
@findex cmacs-gowl-ai-field

With the keyboard in a text field of @emph{any} window---a browser's
comment box, a chat client, a form---one key reads that field, runs a
cmacs-ai text action on it (fix the spelling and grammar, rewrite it,
shorten it, translate it, or whatever you type), and writes the model's
answer straight back into the field.  No Emacs frame opens.  It is the
sibling of the editor (@pxref{Gowl Edit Field}): that one brings the
text to Emacs, this one brings the model to the text.

It needs @command{cmacs --gowl}, for the same reason the editor does:
only the compositor sits between an application and its input method.

@lisp
(add-hook 'cmacs-gowl-mode-hook #'cmacs-gowl-ai-field-setup)
@end lisp

@vindex cmacs-gowl-ai-field-key
@findex cmacs-gowl-ai-field-setup
That binds @code{cmacs-gowl-ai-field-key} (@kbd{Super+Ctrl+a}) in the
compositor.

Five things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{It acts on the selection if there is one, otherwise on the
whole field.}  @xref{Gowl AI Field Scope}.
@item
@strong{The answer is written once.}  The model streams, but an
application cannot take a stream of edits reliably, so the answer is
collected and sent as one edit---one undo step in the application.
@item
@strong{It never types into the wrong window.}  If the keyboard has
moved by the time the answer arrives, or the field no longer holds what
was read, nothing is typed anywhere: the answer goes to the kill ring
and a toast says so.  It never moves focus to make a write possible.
@item
@strong{The text is sent to a model.}  That is the point of it.  What
is refused, and what is deliberately @emph{not} redacted, is under
@ref{Gowl AI Field Privacy}.
@item
@strong{Nothing blocks the desktop.}  Every wait---for the application,
the menu card, the model---is a callback or a timer.
@end itemize

